=== Introduction
--- Psalms 6–8 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalms 6–8
sub: How long, O Lord? • God judgeth the righteous • How excellent is thy name
refs: Psalm 6:3; Psalm 7:11; Psalm 8:1
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse. Press C for the whole chapter.

=== Psalm 6
--- Psalm 6 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 6
sub: Mercy, then judgment • Persistent prayer
refs: Psalm 6:1

--- Rebuke me not in thine anger
kicker: Psalm 6 · Mercy
big: Psalm 6:1 | mark: rebuke me not in thine anger
p: **Psalm 6** continues the prayer and plea theme, with David now up all night with sweat and tears and crying out to the Lord for mercy and for the destruction of his enemies.
p: Appealing to the mercies of the Lord, to not rebuke us in anger, to not chasten us while He is hot and fiery in His wrath, is wisdom that all Christians should take heed of.

--- Heal me; for I am weak
kicker: Psalm 6 · I am weak
big: Psalm 6:2 | mark: for I am weak: O LORD, heal me
p: When we sin, when we have been goaded into a reaction by the temptations in our lives, our first instinct should be to ask God to forgive us.
p: The Devil knows our weaknesses. Our enemies know what buttons to push. How to frustrate us and to get us to react. We are weak. We need God to heal us.

--- Praise, humility, mercy, then judgment
kicker: Psalm 6 · I am weak
p: David is crying out that his body and soul are vexed and he is in real pain, and only the Lord can heal him.
p: Before anything else, the Psalmist has prioritized calling out to God, humbling himself spiritually, physically and mentally, admitting fault and dependency on God. This should be our starting position in prayer.
p: Praise, humility, mercy, then judgment.

--- Deliver us from evil
kicker: Application • Prayer
p: Jesus taught us to pray; lead us not into temptation, but deliver us from evil. To bring an end to the tests and trials, and deliver us from the evil set against us.
p: Many of us may use prayer as a means of complaining, to gripe to God for whatever trouble there is in our life, and to whine about it being unfair that we are suffering at all. I think all Christians can fall into that during our despair. But the problem with this is it lacks the self-reflection and humility, and acknowledgement that we are to blame for many of our own problems. Begging for forgiveness, and forgiving others, recognizing our sin before God, and then asking for relief from His chastening puts the matter right in our heart.

--- But thou, O Lord, how long?
kicker: Psalm 6 · How long?
big: Psalm 6:3 | mark: how long?
p: And so, settling this, David transitions to judgment, asking how long will the Lord allow David's enemies to vex him? How long will the trials and temptations continue?
p: One of the effective means of prayer taught in Psalms, and reinforced by Jesus, is importunity. Persistent prayer. David will frequently ask the Lord to deliver his enemies and ask how long must he suffer the affliction in his life. There is nothing wrong with persistently going to God and asking for His resolution to the matter to come quickly.

--- Men ought always to pray
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 6:3
scripture: Luke 18:1-8 | mark: men ought always to pray, and not to faint

--- Persistent prayer
kicker: Application • Importunity
p: We should not be afraid to bother the Lord with long-standing issues, be it health, or work, or enemies or anything else. Praying for resolution of a matter, while understanding that His will be done, and resigning yourself for whatever the end of the matter is, is crucial in the Christian prayer life.
p: And often, settling it in your heart to be glad with whatever God has given you in terms of an answer will lead to Him blessing you with yet a better alternative or outcome.
p: I have personally experienced this. I have witnessed it in others. Persistent prayer and genuine gratitude for whatever His will is, can often work miracles and bring the Lord to not only give you mercy, but shower you with His grace.

--- For thy mercies' sake
kicker: Psalm 6 · Return, O Lord
scripture: Psalm 6:4-5 | mark: oh save me for thy mercies’ sake
p: Here, David appeals to the promise of God to save us. To remind God of His word shows you actually know it, and trust in it. I think this is pleasing to the Lord.
p: He means the physical death here, but it has the same application spiritually. How are we to praise Him, if we are dead? How can we show the world His mercies if His promises aren't kept.
p: I wonder how many appeals to the throne David sat through, that showed him this pattern of petition.

--- Weary with my groaning
kicker: Psalm 6 · All the night
big: Psalm 6:6 | mark: I am weary with my groaning
p: Letting God know how weary it has become, how the vexation has worn you down, is also an effective means of working it through yourself.
p: David says he is weary with his groaning. He's sick of hearing his own laments. It's all he thinks about, and it is destroying him. The desperate plea to God appeals to His love for us.

--- Depart from me
kicker: Psalm 6 · The Lord hath heard
scripture: Psalm 6:7-8 | mark: Depart from me, all ye workers of iniquity
p: He finishes this Psalm as he has others, letting his enemies know his faith in the Lord is unwavering and his confidence in God is strong.
p: No matter how weary we are, no matter how long we are vexed, no matter how long our temptations, we know we have already overcome in Christ.

--- The Lord will receive my prayer
kicker: Psalm 6 · The Lord hath heard
scripture: Psalm 6:9-10 | mark: the LORD will receive my prayer
p: The Lord has heard our plea. He promised, and so we know He has. He will receive our prayers. Our enemies will know He is with us, when we keep these models of prayer in mind, and humble ourselves to God's will. He will not make us ashamed.

=== Psalm 7
--- Psalm 7 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 7
sub: Search me • God judgeth the righteous • The day of the Lord
refs: Psalm 7:1

--- In thee do I put my trust
kicker: Psalm 7 · Save me
scripture: Psalm 7:1-2 | mark: in thee do I put my trust
p: Again, David writes a prayerful song of faith in God to deliver him from evil. And again, the pattern of praise, plea, confession, and confidence in the Lord is present.
p: Because of their nature, the Psalms are dripping with doctrine. Here we see the faith and confidence in God lends itself to appealing to His mercy and holiness. We learn that we should ask Him to search us. To show us what sins we have in our life, that we may be cleansed and pleasing to Him.

--- If I have done this
kicker: Psalm 7 · If there be iniquity in my hands
scripture: Psalm 7:3-5 | mark: if there be iniquity in my hands
p: David sings out to the Lord to allow the enemies that compass him about to destroy him, if there is anything he had done to deserve this.

--- Self-examination
kicker: Application • Self-examination
p: When we are being persecuted or falling into diverse temptations, tests and trials, self-examination should be on our mind. Dealing honestly with ourselves and with God, that maybe it is the result of something we have done. Chastening of the Lord for iniquity in our dealings with others. Sins against the Father. Confession is rewarded by God. He loves an honest soul, humbly seeking Him for forgiveness.
p: So, like David, call out in crisis, but be willing to accept our role in our calamity, and have a clean conscience towards God. Ask Him to judge you according to your integrity and righteousness, that we don't deceive ourselves. That we don't fall into a victim mentality.

--- My defence is of God
kicker: Psalm 7 · My defence
big: Psalm 7:10 | mark: My defence is of God
p: Pray for God's judgment, not only upon you, but your enemies. That His will be done on earth, and upon all people, that He will stand up the righteous and lay low the wicked. Knowing that our defense is of God.

--- Angry with the wicked every day
kicker: Psalm 7 · God judgeth
big: Psalm 7:11 | mark: God is angry with the wicked every day
p: This important doctrine is a necessary one, to develop a healthy fear of the Lord, the beginning of wisdom.
p: The same wisdom here that we find so often in David's son, Solomon and his Proverbs.

--- The pit he digged
kicker: Psalm 7 · His mischief shall return
scripture: Psalm 7:14-16 | mark: is fallen into the ditch which he made; His mischief shall return upon his own head
p: That those that deal in iniquity will fall victim to their own mischief. The pits they dig for others to fall in will swallow them up. His violence come back upon his own head.

--- He will whet his sword
kicker: Psalm 7 · The wrath of God
scripture: Psalm 7:12-13 | mark: he will whet his sword
p: This expectation of God's judgment is a key portion of our faith. Not only the Lord's mercy upon His children, but His wrath on evil-doers.
p: God is love. But God is also burning vengeance. His anger is kindled and wrath is stored against the wicked.

--- The righteous judgment of God
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 7:11
scripture: Romans 2:2-6 | mark: treasurest up unto thyself wrath against the day of wrath
p: This truth is as important for understanding God as His love. And is why He deserves our praise as we petition Him for our deliverance.

--- I will praise the Lord
kicker: Psalm 7 · Praise
big: Psalm 7:17 | mark: I will praise the LORD according to his righteousness
center: Amen

--- Arise, O Lord, in thine anger
kicker: Psalm 7 · The day of the Lord
scripture: Psalm 7:6-9 | mark: So shall the congregation of the people compass thee about
p: This passage in **Psalms 7** is prophetic to the day of the Lord, Jesus' second coming, the rapture and the wrath.
p: The congregation of the people, the resurrection of the saints, gathered together in the air to meet Him at His glorious coming.

--- The coming of the Son of man
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 7:7
scripture: Matthew 24:27-31 | mark: they shall gather together his elect from the four winds

--- Caught up together
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 7:7
p: As eagles, carrion birds, circle a carcass, so will the risen saints "compass about" Jesus, as we meet Him in the air. When He returns with His own, the spirits of the dead, raising them first, and then those which are alive and remain:
scripture: 1 Thessalonians 4:14-17 | mark: caught up together with them in the clouds, to meet the Lord in the air

--- The day of redemption
kicker: Psalm 7 · The day of the Lord
p: David looked forward to this day of judgement, the day of the Lord. The day of redemption.
p: The pre-trib rapture is so clearly false that it is a wonder it has persisted all this time.
p: "Jesus came to His own. Jesus will come for His own and then Jesus will come with His own!" literally describes 3 comings, yet those who use this will look you in the eye and say that it only describes two.

=== Psalm 8
--- Psalm 8 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 8
sub: The excellent name of the Lord is Jesus
refs: Psalm 8:1

--- How excellent is thy name
kicker: Psalm 8 · The excellent name
big: Psalm 8:1 | mark: how excellent is thy name in all the earth
p: The excellent name of our Lord is Jesus.
p: **Psalm 8** is a song of praise for the Christ. The name of the Lord, who has been set by the {Lord}, God the Father, over all things. Who also is God.

--- Ordained strength
kicker: Psalm 8 · Babes and sucklings
big: Psalm 8:2 | mark: hast thou ordained strength
p: Jesus is the anointed, Who is ordained to strength, and mighty.

--- But we see Jesus
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 8:4–6
scripture: Hebrews 2:4-9 | mark: But we see Jesus, who was made a little lower than the angels

--- Out of the mouth of babes
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 8:2
p: The New Testament reveals the intent and interpretation of the Old. We see Jesus using Psalm 8:2 against the Pharisees, showing that He is the name of God;
scripture: Matthew 21:15-17 | mark: Out of the mouth of babes and sucklings thou hast perfected praise

--- The work of thy fingers
kicker: Psalm 8 · The Creator
big: Psalm 8:3
p: Through His name was all creation made:
scripture: Colossians 1:16-17 | mark: all things were created by him, and for him

--- In the beginning was the Word
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 8:3
scripture: John 1:1-3
p: *The same was in the beginning*, when men first called upon the name of the Lord.
scripture: Genesis 4:25-26 | mark: then began men to call upon the name of the LORD

--- All things under his feet
kicker: Psalm 8 · Dominion
scripture: Psalm 8:6-8 | mark: thou hast put all things under his feet
p: Jesus has been made to have dominion over all things, that the glory and honor be given to Him.
scripture: Ephesians 1:9-10 | mark: he might gather together in one all things in Christ

--- A name which is above every name
kicker: Psalm 8 · The excellent name
p: And the excellency of His name is revealed in Christ Jesus.
scripture: Philippians 2:9-11 | mark: a name which is above every name

--- Before Abraham was, I am
kicker: Psalm 8 · The excellent name
p: Jesus is the name of the Lord. Of the great I Am.
scripture: John 8:56-58 | mark: Before Abraham was, I am
p: No other name is given by God to be worshipped as God.
big: Acts 4:12 | mark: none other name under heaven

--- To put his name there
kicker: Psalm 8 · Where He placed His name
p: Jerusalem, was chosen by God to "place His name";
scripture: Deuteronomy 12:5; Deuteronomy 12:11 | mark: to put his name there; to cause his name to dwell there

--- He was wounded for our transgressions
kicker: Psalm 8 · Where He placed His name
p: The OT scriptures repeatedly say that Jerusalem is where God chose to place His name. Where the Son of God would be revealed. Where His name would be killed for our transgressions:
scripture: Isaiah 53:3-5 | mark: he was wounded for our transgressions

--- The land of Moriah
kicker: Psalm 8 · Where He placed His name
p: The same place Abraham was told to bring Isaac, at Mount Moriah, Zion:
big: Genesis 22:2 | mark: the land of Moriah
p: But God did not require the sacrifice of man, but rather gave His only begotten Son:
big: Genesis 22:8 | mark: God will provide himself a lamb

--- Jehovahjireh
kicker: Psalm 8 · Where He placed His name
p: In this place, where the altar was made, and the son was to be sacrificed, where a ram without blemish was caught in the thick of thorns was provided, Abraham recognized it for what it was:
big: Genesis 22:14 | mark: In the mount of the LORD it shall be seen

--- My name shall be there
kicker: Psalm 8 · Where He placed His name
p: The place where the temple would be, pointing to the Messiah:
scripture: 1 Kings 8:28-29 | mark: My name shall be there

--- For my servant David's sake
kicker: Psalm 8 · Where He placed His name
p: God says that His promise to David that the anointed one would come from his line, and His determined use of Jerusalem as the city where He would place His name, is the only reason He did not wipe out Judah as He did Israel.
scripture: Isaiah 37:34-35 | mark: for my servant David’s sake

--- Thy King cometh unto thee
kicker: Psalm 8 · Where He placed His name
big: Zechariah 9:9 | mark: behold, thy King cometh unto thee
p: There, in Jerusalem would He place His name, Christ Jesus. The Alpha and Omega. The Lord of lords and King of kings.
big: Isaiah 9:6 | mark: his name shall be called Wonderful

--- Concerning me
kicker: Psalm 8 · The name of the Lord is Jesus
p: The entire Old Testament is about Jesus, the Name of the Lord. And so when we see in Moses and the prophets and the Psalms, when they speak of the name of the Lord, we know that it is Jesus.
scripture: Luke 24:44-45 | mark: and in the psalms, concerning me

--- Through his name
kicker: Psalm 8 · The name of the Lord is Jesus
big: Acts 10:43 | mark: through his name whosoever believeth in him shall receive remission of sins
p: This witness is true, and revealed now, in the last times. That the name of the Lord is Jesus. Amen

--- What is man?
kicker: Psalm 8 · A little lower than the angels
scripture: Psalm 8:4-5 | mark: What is man, that thou art mindful of him?; a little lower than the angels
p: **Psalm 8** as a song of praise and worship for the Christ, the Messiah, Who was made a little lower than the angels, and was raised into glory, is a beautiful and heartfelt prayer.
p: When we consider all that the Lord has created and look upon ourselves, we stand in awe that God's grace would ever extend to us. That He would visit us at all, let alone become man and die for our sins.

--- Looking unto Jesus
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 8:5
scripture: Hebrews 12:2-3 | mark: Looking unto Jesus the author and finisher of our faith
p: The awe we should feel in consideration of His grace and mercy - that He became as man, lived, tempted at all points as we are yet without sin, and then despised and brutalized for our sins, and raised again onto glory, that His name be exalted above all - is beautifully summarized in this brief song.

--- O Lord our Lord
kicker: Psalm 8 · How excellent is thy name
big: Psalm 8:9

